"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["school_physics_en"][12]={"school_physics_en:M12-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M12-L1 – Atomic structure and models of matter<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Atoms, nuclear transformations and radiation protection<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A carbon atom can contain six or eight neutrons and still be carbon.\nHow can a model explain that?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>An <strong>atom<\/strong> consists of a very small, positively charged\n<strong>nucleus<\/strong> and a much more extended <strong>electron\ncloud<\/strong>. The nucleus contains positive <strong>protons<\/strong>\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>+<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">+e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nand uncharged <strong>neutrons<\/strong>; the surrounding region contains\nnegative <strong>electrons<\/strong>\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>).\nA neutral atom contains as many electrons as protons. An ion forms when\nelectrons are lost or gained; ordinary chemical reactions do not change\nthe nucleus.<\/p>\n<p>The <strong>atomic number<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the number of protons and defines the chemical element. The\n<strong>mass number<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncounts protons plus neutrons:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=A-Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA <strong>nuclide symbol<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi><\/mi><mi>Z<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msubsup><mi>X<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{A}_{Z}X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nspecifies the element and nuclear composition. <strong>Isotopes<\/strong>\nare atoms of the same element with different neutron numbers. The\nnucleus–electron-cloud model explains basic structure and charge; drawn\ncircular orbits are not literal electron paths. Modern quantum models\ndescribe electron probability distributions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original diagram:<\/strong> a carbon nucleus with six protons\nand six neutrons, accompanied by a schematic distribution of six\nelectrons. Distances and sizes are not to scale.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_070_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic carbon-12 and carbon-14 atoms with six protons, six electrons and different neutron numbers; dimensions not to scale.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic carbon-12 and carbon-14 atoms\nwith six protons, six electrons and different neutron numbers;\ndimensions not to scale.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Atomic number determines the element; isotopes differ in neutron\nnumber.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi><\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mn>14<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{14}_{6}\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>14<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=14<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>14<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=14-6=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA neutral atom has six electrons. In\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi><\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mn>12<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{12}_{6}\\mathrm C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthere are six neutrons instead. Both are carbon isotopes. The nuclide\nsymbol alone does <strong>not<\/strong> specify whether a nucleus is\nstable or hazardous.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Paper modelling:<\/strong> Draw two nuclei with six protons\neach, but six and eight neutrons respectively. Give each neutral atom\nsix electrons. Then change only the electron number to form an ion\nrather than a new element. Do not use radioactive samples.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which property defines a chemical element?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi><\/mi><mn>11<\/mn><mn>23<\/mn><\/msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{23}_{11}\\mathrm{Na}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many electrons are in a neutral atom of sodium with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z=11<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a circular electron orbit only a simplification?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Its proton number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>23<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=23-11=12<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Eleven.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Quantum mechanics describes electron states and probability\ndistributions, not fixed classical trajectories.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The nucleus is enormously smaller than the atom; its size is\nexaggerated in the diagram. Mass number is a <strong>count<\/strong>, not\nan exact mass in kilograms. This simple model cannot quantitatively\nexplain binding energies or spectral lines.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics, grades 7–10: topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Curriculum topic\n3.10 radioactivity and nuclear physics; level differentiation · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://nucleus.iaea.org/sites/nss-oui/Published%20Chunks/m_92a7ef8e-2c5b-4379-8b22-d159e05cd885/c_d4d159a0-7137-49b6-b55d-c8697d094424__13_0.Html\">IAEA\n– Identification of Radioactive Sources and Devices: Definitions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nDefinitions of nuclide, atomic number, alpha/gamma radiation, half-life,\nBq and contamination · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M12-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M12-L2 – Ionising radiation and detection methods<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Atoms, nuclear transformations and radiation protection<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>A detector sometimes clicks even with no visible radioactive object\nbeside it. What does it measure?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Ionising radiation<\/strong> can remove electrons from atoms\nor molecules. <strong>Alpha radiation<\/strong> consists of helium nuclei\n(two protons and two neutrons). <strong>Beta-minus radiation<\/strong>\nconsists of electrons emitted in nuclear transformations. <strong>Gamma\nradiation<\/strong> is high-energy electromagnetic radiation emitted by\nnuclei. X-rays and neutrons can also be ionising; not all ionising\nradiation originates in radioactive decay.<\/p>\n<p>In a <strong>Geiger–Müller counter<\/strong>, ionisation can trigger\nshort electrical pulses. The counter registers events, but cannot by\nitself reliably identify the radiation type or establish a person’s dose\nwithout suitable calibration. The measured <strong>count rate<\/strong>\n(counts per second,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\ndepends on detector properties, geometry, measuring time, energy and\n<strong>background<\/strong>. The <strong>activity<\/strong> of a source\nin becquerels is the number of nuclear transformations per second; the\ntwo quantities must not be equated.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original diagram:<\/strong> schematic alpha, beta and gamma\nemissions reaching a detector, separating radiation type from electronic\ncounts.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_071_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Alpha, beta-minus and gamma radiation shown schematically reaching a detector; measured counts are not activity values.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Alpha, beta-minus and gamma radiation\nshown schematically reaching a detector; measured counts are not\nactivity values.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Detectors register pulses; their rate does not automatically\nequal source activity in becquerels.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A simulated counter records 150 counts in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">60\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA separate background run of the same duration gives 30 counts. The\ntotal count rate is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>150<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>60<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">150/60=2.50\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbackground rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.50<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.50\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand background-subtracted rate in this <strong>simplified model<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.00<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2.00\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNuclear events are random, so repeated measurements fluctuate. This\ncount rate is not a measured activity in Bq.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe data activity:<\/strong> Work with supplied or simulated\ncount tables. Calculate total and background rates and compare\nrepetitions. Do not obtain, open or test radioactive sources\nindependently; any authorised school demonstrations require trained\nstaff and institutional safety procedures.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What kind of radiation consists of helium nuclei?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A simulated detector records 90 counts in 30 s. What is the total\ncount rate?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">If background is 0.6 counts/s, what is the corrected rate?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a detector reading in counts/s not automatically a source\nactivity in Bq?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Alpha radiation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>90<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>30<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3.0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">90/30=3.0\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.0<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.0-0.6=2.4\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Not every decay is detected; efficiency, geometry and background\naffect the observed rate.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Penetration depends on radiation energy and material; simplistic\n“alpha is always completely blocked by paper” claims are not universal.\nA pulse counter alone cannot fully characterise a source or replace dose\nassessment.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics, grades 7–10: topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Curriculum topic\n3.10 radioactivity and nuclear physics; level differentiation · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://nucleus.iaea.org/sites/nss-oui/Published%20Chunks/m_92a7ef8e-2c5b-4379-8b22-d159e05cd885/c_d4d159a0-7137-49b6-b55d-c8697d094424__13_0.Html\">IAEA\n– Identification of Radioactive Sources and Devices: Definitions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nDefinitions of nuclide, atomic number, alpha/gamma radiation, half-life,\nBq and contamination · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.bundesumweltministerium.de/themen/strahlenschutz/ionisierende-strahlung/ueberwachung-der-radioaktivitaet-in-der-umwelt/messanleitungen\">German\nFederal Environment Ministry – Radioactivity measurement guidance<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nEnvironmental monitoring and separate analyses of alpha, beta and gamma\nemitters · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M12-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M12-L3 – Activity, count rate and half-life<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Atoms, nuclear transformations and radiation protection<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why can a source remain active after two half-lives even though three\nquarters of its initially unstable nuclei have decayed?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Radioactive decay<\/strong> of an individual unstable nucleus\nis random. For large numbers of identical nuclei, the average decrease\nis predictable. The <strong>half-life<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_{1/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the time after which half of the initially unstable nuclei remain on\naverage – or the activity has fallen to half. For one radionuclide\nwithout replenishment,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N(t)=N_0\\left(\\frac12\\right)^{t/T_{1/2}},\\qquad A(t)=A_0\\left(\\frac12\\right)^{t/T_{1/2}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the number of undecayed nuclei;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis <strong>activity<\/strong> measured in becquerels\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{Bq}=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnuclear decay per second). A detector’s <strong>count rate<\/strong> is\ndifferent: it depends on detection efficiency, geometry, and background.\nHalf-life depends on the nuclide, not on how much material is initially\npresent. In this ideal model a remainder persists after any finite\ninterval; “everything has decayed after two half-lives” is false.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original diagram:<\/strong> decrease to 100%, 50%, 25% and\n12.5% after zero to three half-lives, clearly identified as a\nstatistical model.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_072_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Exponential model curve showing 100, 50, 25 and 12.5 per cent after zero to three half-lives.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Exponential model curve showing 100, 50,\n25 and 12.5 per cent after zero to three half-lives.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Mean activity halves after each half-life; individual decays are\nrandom.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>A model sample starts at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>800<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_0=800\\,\\mathrm{Bq}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith half-life\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_{1/2}=4\\,\\mathrm h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12\\,\\mathrm h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthree half-lives have elapsed:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>12<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>800<\/mn><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(12\\,\\mathrm h)=800\\left(\\frac12\\right)^3\\,\\mathrm{Bq}=100\\,\\mathrm{Bq}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>After\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\,\\mathrm h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe activity would be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>200<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">200\\,\\mathrm{Bq}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese values are neither detector counts nor dose measurements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Coin simulation:<\/strong> In each round remove all coins\nshowing heads and count how many remain. Repeat with different sample\nsizes and compare individual random runs with the expected trend. No\nionising radiation is required; the model represents statistical\nbehaviour only.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What fraction of the starting activity remains after three\nhalf-lives?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>960<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_0=960\\,\\mathrm{Bq}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_{1/2}=2\\,\\mathrm h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfind\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter 4 h.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many half-lives reduce activity from 800 to 100 Bq?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is source activity not automatically the detected count\nrate?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>%<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/8=12.5\\,\\%<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>960<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>240<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">960/4=240\\,\\mathrm{Bq}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Three.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Detector efficiency, geometry, background and radiation type affect\nobserved counts.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The exponential curve describes the statistical expectation for many\nnuclei. An individual decay time cannot be predicted; relative\nfluctuations are larger for small counts. Decay chains can produce more\ncomplex activity trends.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://nucleus.iaea.org/sites/nss-oui/Published%20Chunks/m_92a7ef8e-2c5b-4379-8b22-d159e05cd885/c_d4d159a0-7137-49b6-b55d-c8697d094424__13_0.Html\">IAEA\n– Identification of Radioactive Sources and Devices: Definitions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nDefinitions of nuclide, atomic number, alpha/gamma radiation, half-life,\nBq and contamination · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics, grades 7–10: topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Curriculum topic\n3.10 radioactivity and nuclear physics; level differentiation · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M12-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M12-L4 – Attenuation and biological effects<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Atoms, nuclear transformations and radiation protection<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>Why can radiation that penetrates poorly from outside still pose a\nserious risk if radioactive matter enters the body?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>Ionising radiation transfers energy to matter and can ionise atoms or\nmolecules. In living cells, damage may affect DNA.\n<strong>Irradiation<\/strong> means that radiation strikes a body;\n<strong>contamination<\/strong> means radioactive material is present on\nor inside it. Exposure alone does not automatically make an object\nradioactive.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Activity<\/strong> in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Bq}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncounts nuclear decays per second. <strong>Absorbed dose<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeasures deposited energy per mass:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm{Gy}=1\\,\\mathrm{J/kg}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Effective dose<\/strong> in sieverts\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Sv}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>)\nis a protection quantity accounting for radiation type and tissue\nsensitivity; it cannot simply be read from source activity or a count\nrate. Health effects depend on radiation type and energy, dose,\nduration, affected tissue and how the material enters the body.<\/p>\n<p>For <strong>external exposure<\/strong>, general principles are\nshorter exposure time, greater distance and suitable shielding. When\n<strong>contamination<\/strong> is possible, preventing intake and\nprofessionally managed decontamination are essential. Shielding reduces\nradiation depending on material, energy and geometry; especially for\ngamma radiation it rarely creates a perfect zero-intensity barrier.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original diagram:<\/strong> short exposure, increased\ndistance, suitable shielding and prevention of intake shown as distinct\nstrategies.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_073_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Two-panel concept: external exposure involving time, distance and shielding versus preventing intake of radioactive substances.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two-panel concept: external exposure\ninvolving time, distance and shielding versus preventing intake of\nradioactive substances.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Radiation protection must distinguish external exposure from\nintake of radioactive material.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>In a purely numerical model with constant absorbed-dose rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>D<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot D=4\\,\\mathrm{\\mu Gy/h}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=0.5\\,\\mathrm h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe <strong>absorbed<\/strong> dose is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mover><mi>D<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=\\dot D\\,t=4\\,\\mathrm{\\mu Gy/h}\\cdot0.5\\,\\mathrm h=2\\,\\mathrm{\\mu Gy}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This classroom example does <strong>not<\/strong> permit direct\nconversion to an effective dose in sieverts or a personal risk\nassessment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Paper activity:<\/strong> Compare three imaginary work plans\nwith different durations at the same assumed dose rate. Draw a barrier\nonly as schematic attenuation. Do not conduct exposure trials, use\nsources or test personal protective arrangements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Distinguish irradiation from contamination.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3\\,\\mathrm{\\mu Gy/h}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nover 2 h in the simplified model.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How do Bq and Gy differ?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a thick barrier alone not a complete safety strategy?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Irradiation means being exposed to radiation; contamination means\nradioactive material on or inside a body.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=6\\,\\mathrm{\\mu Gy}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Bq measures decays per second; Gy measures deposited energy per\nmass.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Radiation energy, type, geometry and intake routes differ;\npreventing contamination is essential when radioactive material is\npresent.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>A short dose-rate calculation assumes constant external exposure.\nProper risk assessment requires radiation and tissue factors; real\ndecisions require qualified personnel and authorities.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L5<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/radiation-ionizing-radiation\">WHO\n– Ionizing radiation: Questions and Answers<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Biological effects,\ndose, exposure and contamination; medical justification · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://nucleus.iaea.org/sites/nss-oui/Published%20Chunks/m_92a7ef8e-2c5b-4379-8b22-d159e05cd885/c_87f5da28-c649-4182-98b4-57d1fc818ba6__2_0.Html\">IAEA\n– Radiation Basics: Effect on Living Tissue<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Ionisation, cellular\ninjury and dose-related effects · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content\nverified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics, grades 7–10: topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Curriculum topic\n3.10 radioactivity and nuclear physics; level differentiation · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M12-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M12-L5 – Nuclear fission, fusion and energy<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Atoms, nuclear transformations and radiation protection<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>The Sun derives much of its energy from fusion; nuclear power plants\nuse fission. What is similar, and what differs?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>In <strong>nuclear fission<\/strong>, a heavy nucleus splits into\nlighter nuclei after suitable excitation. Neutrons and energy may be\nreleased. Some emitted neutrons can cause additional fission; in a\nnuclear reactor the <strong>chain reaction is controlled<\/strong>. In\n<strong>nuclear fusion<\/strong>, lighter nuclei combine into heavier\nones. Getting them close enough requires extreme conditions. Fusion\nreactions provide a major energy source in stars.<\/p>\n<p>Both processes can release <strong>binding energy<\/strong> because\nthe total rest energy of reactants and products differs. The mass–energy\nrelation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><msup><mi>c<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=mc^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexpresses this connection; the mass difference becomes kinetic energy\nand radiation. Electrical power is obtained only through further\nconversions – for example heat, steam and a generator – not from an\nelectrical connection to the nucleus itself.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Original diagram:<\/strong> separated schematic pathways for\nfission and fusion, with explicitly non-scale nuclei.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_074_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic separation of a heavy nucleus into lighter nuclei and neutrons on the left, combination of light nuclei on the right.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic separation of a heavy nucleus\ninto lighter nuclei and neutrons on the left, combination of light\nnuclei on the right.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Fission splits heavy nuclei; fusion joins lighter ones. Both can\nrelease binding energy.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>One <strong>illustrative reaction channel<\/strong> is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi><\/mi><mn>92<\/mn><mn>235<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">U<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mrow><\/mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mn>1<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\"><\/mspace><mo>→<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"><\/mspace><msubsup><mrow><\/mrow><mn>56<\/mn><mn>141<\/mn><\/msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mrow><\/mrow><mn>36<\/mn><mn>92<\/mn><\/msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msubsup><mrow><\/mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mn>1<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{235}_{92}\\mathrm U+{}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm n\\;\\longrightarrow\\;{}^{141}_{56}\\mathrm{Ba}+{}^{92}_{36}\\mathrm{Kr}+3\\,{}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm n+E.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Mass numbers:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>235<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>236<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">235+1=236<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non the left,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>141<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>92<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>236<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">141+92+3=236<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non the right. Atomic numbers:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>92<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>56<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>36<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">92=56+36<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nActual fission fragments vary; this is only <strong>one possible\nchannel<\/strong>. A common fusion reaction is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi><\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mrow><\/mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><msubsup><mrow><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mn>4<\/mn><\/msubsup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>+<\/mo><msubsup><mrow><\/mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mn>1<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">^{2}_{1}\\mathrm H+{}^{3}_{1}\\mathrm H\\rightarrow{}^{4}_{2}\\mathrm{He}+{}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm n+E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Safe paper or block model:<\/strong> Represent each nuclear\nreactant and product with numbered cards, verify\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non both sides, and map the energy conversion chain in a power plant. Do\nnot use radioactive substances, neutron sources or high-energy\nequipment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which reaction joins two light nuclei?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Verify the sum of mass numbers for the example fission\nreaction.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is grid electricity not produced directly by a nuclear\nreaction?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does every fission event produce precisely the same fragments?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Nuclear fusion.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Left 236; right\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>141<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>92<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>236<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">141+92+3=236<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Released nuclear energy must be converted, commonly through heat and\na generator, into electrical energy.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; the possible fission channels and products vary.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>The mass-number balance describes one possible fission channel;\nenergy production cannot be computed merely by adding nucleon masses\nwithout binding-energy information. A short schematic cannot assess\noverall reactor performance. Fusion research should not be confused with\nestablished commercially operating fusion power stations.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Next lesson:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M12&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M12-L6<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.energy.gov/ne/articles/fission-and-fusion-what-difference\">US\nDepartment of Energy – Fission and Fusion: What is the Difference?<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nFission, released neutrons, chain reactions and electricity generation ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.energy.gov/science/doe-explainsdeuterium-tritium-fusion-fuel\">US\nDepartment of Energy – Deuterium–Tritium Fusion Fuel<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nDeuterium–tritium reaction to helium and neutron; research status · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://bildungsserver.berlin-brandenburg.de/rlp-online/c-faecher/physik/themen-und-inhalte\">Berlin-Brandenburg\neducation server – Physics, grades 7–10: topics<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Curriculum topic\n3.10 radioactivity and nuclear physics; level differentiation · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","school_physics_en:M12-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M12-L6 – Medical uses, risks and radioactive-waste management<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M12 – Atoms, nuclear transformations and radiation protection<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Starting question<\/h2>\n<p>How can the same physical phenomenon aid cancer treatment yet require\nsafeguards lasting many generations?<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical concepts and model<\/h2>\n<p>Ionising radiation has distinct <strong>medical\napplications<\/strong>: <strong>diagnostic<\/strong> procedures such as\nX-ray imaging and CT produce images; <strong>nuclear medicine<\/strong>\nuses suitable radionuclides for diagnosis or therapy;\n<strong>radiotherapy<\/strong> can target cancer cells. Clinical\ndecisions must justify expected benefit against radiation risk and\nappropriately optimise patient exposure. These are regulated clinical\nprocedures, not school experiments.<\/p>\n<p>Radioactive waste arises in nuclear technology, research and medical\nuse. Its hazards depend not just on <strong>activity<\/strong> but also\non radionuclides, half-lives, heat generation, chemical form and\npotential release pathways. <strong>Interim storage<\/strong> is\nsupervised storage pending further management; <strong>final\ndisposal<\/strong> aims at safe containment over very long periods. In\nGermany, site selection for high-level radioactive waste involves\nscientific investigations, geological barriers, engineered packages and\npublic participation. <strong>As of November 2026, a final site for the\nplanned high-level waste repository has not yet been\nselected.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p><strong>Original diagram:<\/strong> medical benefit/risk assessment\nalongside multiple conceptual geological safety barriers, without\nrepresenting an actual repository site.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-school-physics-en/media/img/S_PHY_075_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Medical imaging and therapy alongside a generic multilayered rock and engineered container concept for waste isolation.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Medical imaging and therapy alongside a\ngeneric multilayered rock and engineered container concept for waste\nisolation.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Medical radiation requires benefit–risk assessment; disposal\nrequires substantiated long-term safety.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example or application<\/h2>\n<p>Consider two information cards: (A) a clinically indicated CT scan\nwith a diagnostic benefit, and (B) an unnecessary repeat with no\nadditional clinical information. For A, expected benefit and exposure\noptimisation may justify the examination; B should be questioned. For\ndisposal, “the rock is thick” is not enough: long-term safety depends on\ngeological characterisation, groundwater pathways, container design and\nindependent assessment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Safe observation and experiment<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Evidence-based classroom discussion:<\/strong> In three groups\nrepresent patient protection, scientific safety and affected\ncommunities. Use the linked primary sources to identify what evidence is\nrequired before a medical procedure or a site decision. Do not make\npatient-specific dose decisions or handle radioactive waste.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Check your learning<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"questions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Questions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Give one diagnostic and one therapeutic use of ionising\nradiation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which principle governs medical irradiation?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is half-life not the sole criterion for judging radioactive\nwaste?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does interim storage differ from final disposal?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"answers\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Answers<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For example CT imaging for diagnosis and radiotherapy for cancer\ntreatment.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The clinical procedure must be justified and the exposure\noptimised.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Amount, radionuclides, physical/chemical form, heat and possible\nrelease pathways also matter.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">4.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Interim storage is controlled holding pending further management;\nfinal disposal aims at very long-term safe containment.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Disposal plans can change with scientific studies and legal\ndecisions; a school diagram cannot substitute for a site assessment.\nMedical indications and dose decisions require qualified clinicians.\nContamination, health and environmental concerns require specialist\nanalysis.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Looking ahead:<\/strong> The next module begins with <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=school_physics&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L1 – Vectors, scalars and\ncomponents<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/radiation-ionizing-radiation\">WHO\n– Ionizing radiation: Questions and Answers<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Biological effects,\ndose, exposure and contamination; medical justification · Last accessed:\n2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.bundesumweltministerium.de/themen/strahlenschutz/ionisierende-strahlung/strahlenschutz-in-der-medizin\">German\nFederal Environment Ministry – Radiation protection in medicine<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nMedical benefit-risk considerations, justification and optimisation ·\nLast accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.bge.de/de/endlagersuche/fragen-und-antworten/\">BGE –\nQuestions and answers about Germany’s repository site selection<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nRepository search progress, geological suitability and public\nparticipation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.bundesumweltministerium.de/faq/wo-sollen-die-radioaktiven-abfaelle-endgelagert-werden\">German\nFederal Environment Ministry – Where will radioactive waste be disposed\nof?<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Planned disposal pathways and long-term safety goal for\nhigh-level waste · Last accessed: 2026-11-21 · Content verified:\n2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
